@@ -117,0 +118,43 @@ ros2 launch fast_lio mapping.launch.py config_file:=avia.yaml
+### 3.1.1 Transitional downstream-backend mode
+
+`mapping.launch.py` can expose FAST-LIO's native point-to-plane correspondence
+packet to a downstream unified backend while suppressing FAST-LIO pose outputs:
+
+```bash
+ros2 launch fast_lio mapping.launch.py \
+  config_file:=mid360.yaml \
+  downstream_backend_enable:=true
+```
+
+When this mode is enabled, native factor export is forced on. `/Odometry`,
+`/path`, and the `camera_init -> body` TF are disabled by default so they are
+not mistaken for the authoritative fused state. They can be enabled separately
+for comparison only:
+
+```bash
+downstream_publish_diagnostic_odometry:=true
+downstream_publish_diagnostic_path:=true
+downstream_publish_diagnostic_tf:=true
+```
+
+This is a compatibility mode, not a complete transfer of estimator or map
+ownership. FAST-LIO still runs its internal ESIKF and updates its ikd-Tree map
+to obtain correspondences. The downstream backend must not treat the optional
+FAST-LIO pose diagnostics as an independent measurement alongside the exported
+LiDAR and IMU factors.
+
+Map insertion ownership can be transferred separately during this transition:
+
+```bash
+ros2 launch fast_lio mapping.launch.py \
+  config_file:=mid360.yaml \
+  downstream_backend_enable:=true \
+  downstream_map_insertion_mode:=backend_confirmed \
+  downstream_backend_state_topic:=/fusion/unified/odom
+```
+
+The first scan still bootstraps the ikd-Tree coordinate frame. Every later
+downsampled, deskewed scan is queued and inserted only after a same-timestamp
+optimized backend pose arrives. This does not yet bypass the iterated ESIKF
+update; it changes map insertion ownership only.
+
